# git.commit.set

commits as seaturtle[bot], with the human co-author credited
```bash
printf 'type(scope): summary\n\n- detail' | rhx git.commit.set -m @stdin --mode apply
```
message: line 1 header, line 2 blank, lines 3+ body — all required
options: `--mode plan` (default, preview) · `--mode apply` · `--push` (push + findsert pr) · `--unstaged ignore` (staged only) · `--unstaged include` (stage all first)

grant: ask your sponsor; it runs `git.commit.uses set --quant N` — covers commit, stage, rebase, and push to the own branch; a grant at 0 left still pushes. release is its own meter: `git.release.uses set --quant 1` lets `git.release --mode apply` ship, granted the same way; a commit grant never ships

sponsor: on the human's own machine, git config names them, no bind needed. a bind wins over git config; on a clone's machine (git config names a seaturtle identity) a human binds one at a real terminal: `rhx git.commit.sponsor set --who <@stdin|@self|"Name <email>">`

